RepriceService:
```php
final class RepriceService
{
    public function __construct(private EntityManagerInterface $em, private PriceRowWriter $priceRowWriter, private BillingRepository $repo, private RateCalculator $calc) {}

    public function reprice(string $json): array // [count:int, delta:string(formatted 6)]
    {
        $data = json_decode($json,true,128,JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR);
        if (!is_array($data)) throw new RuntimeException(...);
        $prices = Validators::list($data, 'prices');
        $touchedIds = [];
        foreach ($prices as $raw) {
            if (!is_array($raw)) throw ...
            $row = $this->priceRowWriter->upsert($raw);
            if (null !== $row->getId()) $touchedIds[] = $row->getId();
        }
        $events = $this->repo->findRatedEventsByPriceRowIds($touchedIds);
        $oldSum = '0'; $newSum = '0'; $n = 0;
        foreach ($events as $event) {
            $oldSum = DecimalMath::add($oldSum, $event->getCost() ?? '0');
            $row = $event->getPriceRow();
            if (null === $row) continue;
            $offPeak = OffPeakWindow::fromProvider($row->getProvider());
            $newCost = $this->calc->calculate($event, $row, $offPeak);
            $event->setCost($newCost->amount());
            $newSum = DecimalMath::add($newSum, $newCost->amount());
            $n++;
        }
        $this->em->flush();
        $delta = DecimalMath::format6(DecimalMath::sub($newSum, $oldSum));
        return [$n, $delta];
    }
}
```

Wait — the upsert in PriceRowWriter requires the provider + model to exist (findProviderByCode / findModelByProviderCodeAndCode). For reprice, the spec says provider & model must exist in DB (not necessarily in the file). My PriceRowWriter checks existence in DB — good. If the reprice file references a provider/model not in DB → DomainException → exit 1. Good.

One concern: PriceRowWriter uses `findProviderByCode` (must exist) and `findModelByProviderCodeAndCode` (must exist). For reprice this matches the requirement. Good.

But there's a subtlety: In reprice, after upserting (which may create NEW price rows with new ids), I collect touchedIds including new row ids. Then I query events whose priceRow is IN touchedIds. New rows have no events → no recompute for them. Existing rows (replaced) have events → recomputed. Good.

Also, the priceRowWriter upsert for an existing row replaces rates (delete + insert new PriceRate). The row id stays. Events reference the row id → still valid. Good.

