content.thinking:
The mapping has `orphanRemoval: true` and `cascade: ['persist', 'remove']`. So `removeElement` SHOULD delete orphaned rows. But it didn't work on re-import.

Why? The issue: when the existing PriceRow is LOADED from DB (via findPriceRow), its `rates` collection is initialized (lazy). `replaceRates` iterates `$this->rates` (loads children), removes each (marking them orphan → for deletion), then adds new PriceRate entities. On flush, Doctrine should: DELETE the orphans and INSERT the new ones. 

But the error is a duplicate key violation on INSERT. So the DELETE isn't happening before the INSERT, OR the orphans aren't being deleted.

Hmm. The problem might be the ORDER of SQL operations: Doctrine executes INSERTs before DELETEs sometimes, causing the duplicate. Actually, Doctrine typically orders: INSERT (new), then UPDATE, then DELETE — no, it's usually persistent collection persister handles it. Actually the standard SQL execution order in UnitOfWork is: INSERTs, then UPDATEs for new-to-existing, then ... and collection deletions. The issue: inserting new (row_id=1, kind=input) while old (row_id=1, kind=input) still exists → duplicate on INSERT before DELETE.

This is a known Doctrine gotcha: when replacing children with new identities and unique constraint on (parent, child-kind), the INSERT happens before the DELETE → constraint violation.

The robust fix: don't create NEW PriceRate entities each time; instead, REUSE existing PriceRate entities by kind and just UPDATE their rate value. Only create new if the kind is missing; only remove if a kind is no longer wanted. Since re-import uses the SAME unit (same kinds), the kinds match, so I just update the rate in place — no insert, no delete → no unique conflict.

Let me rewrite `replaceRates` to match by kind and update in place:

```php
public function replaceRates(array $rates): self
{
    $desiredKinds = array_keys($rates);

    // existing rates mapped by kind
    $existingByKind = [];
    foreach ($this->rates as $rate) {
        $existingByKind[$rate->getKind()] = $rate;
    }

    // remove kinds no longer desired (orphan removal)
    foreach ($existingByKind as $kind => $rate) {
        if (!in_array($kind, $desiredKinds, true)) {
            $this->rates->removeElement($rate);
        }
    }

    // update or insert each desired rate
    foreach ($rates as $kind => $value) {
        if (isset($existingByKind[$kind])) {
            $existingByKind[$kind]->setRate($value);   // update in place
        } else {
            $rate = new PriceRate();
            $rate->setPriceRow($this);
            $rate->setKind($kind);
            $rate->setRate($value);
            $this->rates->add($rate);
        }
    }

    return $this;
}
```

This update-in-place approach avoids the INSERT-before-DELETE collision for the common case (same kinds). And for the change-kind case, orphan removal handles deletion. But if kinds change AND new kinds added, still could collide... but that's an edge case; the fixtures don't change kinds. For robustness, the in-place update is the right fix.

But wait — there's still a subtlety: on re-import, `$this->rates` must be LOADED (materialized) to see existing children. Doctrine lazy-loads on first access. In replaceRates I iterate `$this->rates` → triggers lazy load of managed collection → good. For a NEW row (first import), the collection is empty → all inserted. Good.

However, there's a catch: for the UPDATE-in-place to work across the re-import, the PriceRow loaded from DB must have its rates association already managed. When I call findPriceRow, it returns a managed entity. Accessing ->rates triggers a SELECT for children. Then I mutate. On flush, the existing PriceRate entities (now modified via setRate) get UPDATE statements. No inserts, no deletes for same-kind. 

